"Accumulates departures from a stable target to detect persistent small shifts that individual-value charts may miss.\n\nUse when: Small sustained process shifts matter and a stable reference distribution is available. Detection sensitivity and false-alarm behavior can be configured explicitly.\n\nAvoid when: The baseline drifts naturally or serial dependence is ignored in the monitoring design.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes observation order. Vertical position encodes cumulative standardized or unstandardized deviations under the declared CUSUM rule.\n\nBuild: Define target, baseline variation, and the shift size the monitor should detect. Choose a documented one-sided, two-sided, or V-mask formulation with reference and decision parameters. Compute the cumulative statistics in sequence and mark threshold crossings without rewriting history.\n\nCheck: Check standardization and subgroup-size assumptions match the baseline. Verify reference value, decision interval, and reset policy are explicit. Assess false-alarm and detection behavior for the stated process assumptions.\n\nPitfalls: A CUSUM is not simply a cumulative total of production quantities. Do not select thresholds retrospectively just to highlight a desired change; annotate interventions and any deliberate resets.\n\nExample: Monitor synthetic fill-weight deviations with a two-sided tabular CUSUM calibrated for a small sustained mean shift.\n\nAlternatives: EWMA chart, Individuals control chart, Residual sequence plot."
